>> title: Chāshū
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: pork, japanese, braise, ramen, make-ahead
>> counters: Ramen Shop, The Bowl Shop
>> aka: chashu, chāshū, char siu pork for ramen, チャーシュー, 叉焼, rolled pork belly, braised pork belly for ramen, ramen pork, nibuta
>> pairs-with: shoyu-tare, tonkotsu-ramen, shoyu-ramen, shio-ramen, miso-ramen
>> servings: 8
>> time: 12 hr
>> slack: forgiving — three hours at a tremble is three hours or three and a half, and the belly only gets softer; the eight-hour chill in its own liquid is not optional though, because warm belly tears under a knife and there is no slicing it thin until it is cold
>> step.1: The braising liquid comes out of this a tare — one preparation, two jobs, and both are worth keeping.
>> step.2: roll skin-out, tie at 1-in intervals
>> step.3: brown all over 8 min
>> step.4: braise 3 hr at a tremble, turning
>> step.5: chill in the liquid 8 hr, then slice thin

The braising liquid comes out of this a tare — one preparation, two jobs, and both are worth keeping.

Roll @pork belly{3%lb}(1.35 kg; skinless, in one rectangular slab) into a tight log with the grain running the length of it, rub it with @kosher salt{2%tsp}(10 g), and tie it at 1-inch intervals with #kitchen twine{}. Tied loosely it unrolls in the pot and the slices fall apart; tied properly it cuts into rounds that hold together on top of a bowl.

Brown @&(~1)tied pork{} in @neutral oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) in a heavy #Dutch oven{} ~brown{8%min}, turning it onto every face until the whole outside is deep gold.

Braise @&(~1)browned pork{} in @soy sauce{1%cup}(240 mL), @sake{1%cup}(240 mL), @mirin{1/2%cup}(120 mL), @granulated sugar{1/3%cup}(70 g), @garlic{6%cloves}(crushed), @fresh ginger{2%in}(sliced), @scallions{3%stalks} and @water{2%cups}(480 mL), covered, at a bare tremble ~braise{3%hr}, turning it every 45 minutes so every face spends time under the liquid. It is done when a skewer goes through the middle with no resistance at all.

Chill @&(~1)chāshū{} in its liquid ~chill{8%hr}, then lift the log out, untie it and slice it into 1/8-inch rounds — warm belly tears under a knife and cold belly cuts clean, so this is not a step to hurry. Strain the liquid it sat in and reduce it by half: that is a chāshū tare, and two spoonfuls of it will season a bowl on its own.
